The longer chronology records the disagreements among witnesses, including events that may have been revised after they were remembered.<ref>[[Sovereign/History|History]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#Failures and anti-feats|Feats, “Failures and anti-feats”]].</ref>

== The world he found ==

Leth is not presented as a stack of higher and higher universes. The archive describes a '''world''' of lived events, an '''account''' that gives those events their permitted shape, a '''reading''' that makes an account actual, and a '''witness''' that preserves a difference between one state and another. A person may occupy more than one of these roles: Sana is part of Leth's world, but her incompatible memories also witness two readings of it. None of the four terms names a universally highest floor. They are ways the story keeps track of what changed and who can tell.<ref>[[Sovereign/Cosmology|Cosmology]]; [[Sovereign/Self-Refinement#Operational sequence|Self-Refinement, “Operational sequence”]].</ref>

Ivo discovered this structure through administrative mistakes rather than revelation. The uncaused bell had produced open doors and wet coats even though its cause was absent. On another day Sana answered a question he had not yet asked; Ivo found the question already written in the margin of a survey proof. He did not suddenly know that all worlds were books. He knew only that, in Leth, a description could arrive before the event it described and that a damaged description did not always remove the event's consequences. He had to learn how to read that damage before he could touch it.<ref>[[Sovereign/History#The Reader|History, “The Reader”]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#2. The unasked question — temporal|Feats, “The unasked question”]].</ref>

Later, an account treated the whole history of Leth as a cautionary fable. This did not make its people unreal to one another. It made the relation between accounts another place where omission and disagreement could occur. Ivo's change at that stage was to carry a witness across the relation without erasing the difference between the two tellings. Sana's memories did not harmonize; one version of her remembered a relationship the other had never lived. The apparent ascent was also a division Ivo could not repair for her.<ref>[[Sovereign/History#The Recursive Witness|History, “The Recursive Witness”]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#10. The fable of Leth — recursive|Feats, “The fable of Leth”]].</ref>

== Abilities and method ==

'''The measure''' is the narrow brass frame Ivo used to align two printed maps. After the first anomalies it began revealing differences between descriptions: a line moved, a word omitted, a name appearing only in one witness's account. It does not tell him which account is true. Its importance is practical and moral. It slows him down long enough to see that a correction has a cost.<ref>[[The Measure]]; [[Sovereign/Abilities#The measure|Abilities, “The measure”]].</ref>

'''Readdressing''' is his best known defense. Most effects require some way of determining what they act upon, even if the address is as broad as “anything.” Ivo can sometimes change his own relation to that address. In the famous absolute-attack episode, the attack was not overpowered. It finished under its own rules, and the question became whether its completed target was still Ivo. The tribunal case shows why this is more unsettling than an immunity: the named defendant died. A surviving name is not proof of a surviving consciousness.<ref>[[Sovereign/Abilities#Readdressing|Abilities, “Readdressing”]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#9. The Absolute Attack — semantic|Feats, “The Absolute Attack”]].</ref>

'''Witness transfer''' allows a difference retained in one account to inform a revision in another, when a real relation joins them. '''Margin Concordance''' lets conflicting accounts remain legible beside one another. Neither grants Ivo ownership of them. These practices are most useful when one telling has been made official and another remembers what it left out. They are also dangerous: once he chooses which trace counts as the bridge, he may impose his preferred history on someone who did not ask for it.<ref>[[Sovereign/Abilities#Witness transfer|Abilities, “Witness transfer”]]; [[Sovereign/Abilities#Margin Concordance|Abilities, “Margin Concordance”]].</ref>

'''The Deferred Ending''' holds a declared conclusion open long enough to test whether it follows from its stated premises. It cannot make completed harm unhappen. '''The Last Word''' forces a supposedly unconditional operation to finish stating what it acts on and how. These are not incantations that embarrass a stronger opponent into defeat. They matter when the opponent's own action depends on a target or relation its claim has left unspecified. When no such gap exists, Ivo has nothing to use.<ref>[[Sovereign/Abilities#The Deferred Ending|Abilities, “The Deferred Ending”]]; [[Sovereign/Abilities#The Last Word|Abilities, “The Last Word”]].</ref>

'''The Unwritten State''' is the most frightening extension of his self-editing. He can withdraw a part of his description while its consequences remain: warmth in an empty chair, a promise with no remembered recipient, an unfinished grammatical place. The missing part is not a secret weapon waiting to be named. If Ivo withdraws too much, there may be no witness left to establish that the person who returns is him. He once came close enough to that edge that the archive will not fill the blank on his behalf.<ref>[[The Unwritten State]]; [[Sovereign/Abilities#The Unwritten State|Abilities, “The Unwritten State”]].</ref>

'''Counterfactual Mercy''' is an ethical practice rather than an attack. Ivo shows a harmed person two possible repairs, including the price of each, and allows refusal. It arose after he nearly changed Sana's memory under a rule that mistook silence for consent. It cannot solve emergencies in which there is no time to ask, and it cannot prove that a restored person is numerically identical with the one lost. It does make Ivo's power answerable to someone other than Ivo.<ref>[[Sovereign/Abilities#Counterfactual Mercy|Abilities, “Counterfactual Mercy”]]; [[Sovereign/Philosophy#Consent and Counterfactual Mercy|Philosophy, “Consent and Counterfactual Mercy”]].</ref>

'''The Prior Answer''' lets Ivo form a conditional revision before a threat removes every later trace. [[The White Interval]] records a closing force that killed the prior Ivo and erased his witnesses, yet failed to complete its wider erasure when the prepared succession resolved. '''The Unruled Witness''' goes further: it can self-revise without requiring a prior attack, a substrate, or another account's permission. In this state, he can select a fictional outcome without the ordinary chain of force and counterforce. The [[The Unconditioned Draft|peak-form record]] treats that reach as frightening precisely because Ivo knows what it would mean to make every other answer his own.<ref>[[The White Interval]]; [[The Unconditioned Draft]]; [[Sovereign/Abilities#The Prior Answer|Abilities, “The Prior Answer”]].</ref>

== Recorded encounters ==

'''The bell.''' This first correction is easy to mistake for a clean rescue. No child drowned. Yet the previous rescue also vanished from the mother's memory. The episode established the pattern that follows Ivo: an improved outcome can erase the relationship by which someone understood what happened.

'''The tribunal.''' A later order was written to execute its defendant without exception. Ivo found the address by which the order identified him and revised his relation to it. The order was not weakened or blocked. Its named defendant died. A later Ivo spoke, but the archive cannot prove that the dead person continued rather than being replaced by a perfect successor. The event demonstrates a narrow method against an absolute effect and the terrible uncertainty hidden by the word “survived.”<ref>[[Sovereign/Feats#5. The lethal order — identity|Feats, “The lethal order”]]; [[Sovereign/Self-Refinement#The address of an absolute attack|Self-Refinement, “The address of an absolute attack”]].</ref>

'''The finished book.''' The Closed King controlled every event and agent in a completed account. Ivo did not depose it. He found a relation between that account and the reading that declared it finished, then asked whether completion belonged to the world or to the reader's practice. The King remained absolute where its authority had been established. What the encounter demonstrated was a possible opening between an account and its interpretation, not superiority over every conceivable creator.<ref>[[The Closed King]]; [[Sovereign/History#Later disputed episodes|History, “Later disputed episodes”]].</ref>

'''The missing place.''' In the Unwritten State, Ivo withdrew part of his own description. The room retained the warmth of the chair he had occupied; a friend remembered having promised someone something but not to whom. These are bounded effects of an absence, not proof that the missing part is immune to everything. Ivo came close to leaving too little for anyone to identify his continuation.<ref>[[The Unwritten State]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#16. The warm chair — unwritten|Feats, “The warm chair”]].</ref>

'''The unfinished mercy.''' A presence able to complete every account offered to repair the losses Ivo could not. It had no exterior he could escape to; his usual techniques failed. Ivo held two accounts of Sana's life beside one another and gave up the authority to choose her answer. She refused a future in which she could never answer again. The presence withdrew rather than complete a city by calling coercion mercy. Ivo defeated its plan and kept no claim to its power. The flood resumed; Teren still did not know him. [[The Last Mercy]] preserves the scene and its cost.<ref>[[The Last Mercy]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#17. The unfinished mercy — consent|Feats, “The unfinished mercy”]].</ref>

'''The white interval.''' An indifferent force closed the office, its people, and the accounts that could testify to their loss. Ivo prepared a revision before it reached him. The prior Ivo ended; a successor interrupted the force's next act. The victory saved Leth from completion without restoring everyone already taken. [[The White Interval]] makes the event and its identity cost explicit.<ref>[[The White Interval]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#18. The white interval — prior answer|Feats, “The white interval”]].</ref>

'''The court of endings.''' Final authorities from incompatible accounts tried to assign him a last description. As the Unruled Witness, Sovereign could have selected a verdict defeating every opposing voice. He instead ended their claim over him and left their worlds intact. [[The Unconditioned Draft]] preserves the scene because the unused verdict explains both the peak's reach and the choice that makes him more than its reach.<ref>[[The Unconditioned Draft#The court of endings|The Unconditioned Draft, “The court of endings”]]; [[Sovereign/Feats#19. The court of endings — self-jurisdiction|Feats, “The court of endings”]].</ref>

The complete [[Sovereign/Feats|feat register]] records nineteen events, their evidence, the proposed mechanism, and what each event fails to establish. The final entry shows the Unruled Witness declining an effortless victory in the court of endings. The archive also records defeats and failures rather than treating a scholar's explanation as more certain than an event.

== The thing he leaves alone ==

Ivo remembers a blackout in Tala's kitchen. Rain crossed the window. Mira balanced a paper boat on a chipped cup and said, "Your turn to dry." The cup is green in one recollection, blue in another. Someone may have been humming outside the room, although no record places a fourth person in the apartment.

He could settle the inconsistencies by editing the memory. He will not. [[The Anchor Memory]] may be the last part of his life he did not choose, or a later invention he has mistaken for one. Neither answer has been proved. His refusal has saved nothing by itself; at times it has made saving someone harder. It remains the one promise he knows he is making now.<ref>[[The Anchor Memory#Discrepancies|The Anchor Memory, “Discrepancies”]] and [[The Anchor Memory#The cost|“The cost”]].</ref>

== What can be lost ==

Ivo can evade many kinds of destruction. He cannot decide for another person that an altered life is the same life. Teren survives one repair and no longer knows him. A restored man carries a dead man's memories but cannot recognize his husband. Sana refuses a correction Ivo believes would spare her pain. The archive does not treat these as small costs paid for larger feats. They are the story's measure of him.<ref>[[Sovereign/Relationships|Relationships]]; [[Sovereign/Philosophy#Consent and Counterfactual Mercy|Philosophy, “Consent and Counterfactual Mercy”]].</ref>

His central flaw has not disappeared. He still wants to make a wrong thing right before anyone has finished telling him what the wrong thing meant. Sana's refusal teaches him that restraint can be care, though she also reminds him that refusing to help is itself a choice with consequences. He cannot resolve that tension by growing stronger. When he calls himself the Open Witness, the title records a practice of leaving testimony available, including testimony against him. It is neither absolution nor retirement.<ref>[[Sovereign/Philosophy#The right to revise|Philosophy, “The right to revise”]]; [[Sovereign/History#The Open Witness|History, “The Open Witness”]].</ref>

The [[The Closed King|Closed King]] keeps complete authority in a finished account; Ivo does not take its throne. In the [[The Reader Problem|Reader Problem]], different interpretations leave different Ivos answerable for different acts. [[Sovereign/Philosophy]] asks when the ability to revise becomes an obligation, and when it becomes an intrusion.

== Appearance ==

He has uneven dark hair, a pale fleck near his right pupil, and the habit of leaning toward whoever is speaking. His indigo coat has a copper-darned cuff. On a cord he carries [[The Measure|a brass registration frame]] from the corrections office. When he revises himself, straight lines briefly fail to meet their shadows. Nobody has established whether that sign belongs to Ivo or to a witness trying to keep track of him.

== Reading the archive ==

'''Canon events''' are the incidents recorded in [[Sovereign/History]] and [[Sovereign/Feats]]. '''In-universe theories''' include the axioms, which their own authors dispute. '''Comparative interpretations''' appear in [[Sovereign/Comparative model]]; they are arguments about possible encounters with other kinds of fiction, not events in Ivo's life. [[The Last Mercy]] and [[The White Interval]] show two different totalizing threats. [[The Unconditioned Draft]] explains the peak state that the archivists' ordinary measurements could not describe.

The strongest comparative case uses the Unruled Witness, not merely the clerk's responsive technique. An opposing being may be absolute within its own reality; that fact does not automatically give it authority over Sovereign's self-definition. The White Interval also shows why erasure of every ''later'' clue does not prove that no change could resolve ''during'' the attack. These are reasons to analyze the encounter rather than awarding either side victory from a title. Within Sovereign's canon, the peak can make victory its chosen outcome; the story remains concerned with why he rarely chooses to do so.<ref>[[The Unconditioned Draft]]; [[Sovereign/Comparative model#What would count as an answer|Comparative model, “What would count as an answer”]].</ref>

